PAGE = dict(
    slug='ribbon',
    title='Pane::ribbon',
    signature='Trace& Pane::ribbon(Series y, double base = 0.0)\nTrace& Pane::ribbon(Series x, Series y, double base = 0.0)',
    intro='Adds a **filled band**: the region between a baseline and a curve through the values, the trace of an area chart. The baseline is `base`, 0 by default, so a positive series is filled down to the axis; a different `base` is a different floor.\n\n| Call | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `ribbon(y, base = 0)` | Values at the positions 1, 2, 3, ... |\n| `ribbon(x, y, base = 0)` | Values at the positions `x`. |\n\nA rank-2 `y` is read as one series per column, with a band for each, stacked one on top of the next. `x` and `y` have as many rows as each other. For the same data as vertical stems rather than a filled region use [`spike`](spike.md); for the line alone, [`trend`](trend.md).',
    parameters='**x** (`Series`-convertible, second call)\n    The positions.\n\n**y** (`Series`-convertible)\n    The values.\n\n**base** (`double`, default 0)\n    The baseline the band start from.',
    returns='`Trace&`\n    The new trace.',
    examples=[
        dict(id='y', text='Values only: the positions are the indices 1, 2, 3, ... The region between the values and the base (0) is filled.',
             code='auto fig = studio("ribbon_y");\nauto& pane = fig.pane2("p");\npane.ribbon(std::vector<double>{2, 4, 3, 5, 4, 6});\nfig.save_svg("figure.svg");'),
        dict(id='xy', text='Positions and values. The positions need not be evenly spaced.',
             code='auto fig = studio("ribbon_xy");\nauto& pane = fig.pane2("p");\npane.ribbon(std::vector<double>{0, 1, 3, 4, 8, 9}, std::vector<double>{2, 4, 3, 5, 4, 6});\nfig.save_svg("figure.svg");'),
        dict(id='base', text='The base moves the baseline. With base 3 the band is drawn between 3 and each value, so values below 3 fill upward from the line to the data.',
             code='auto fig = studio("ribbon_base");\nauto& pane = fig.pane2("p");\npane.ribbon(std::vector<double>{2, 4, 3, 5, 4, 6}, 3.0);\npane.rule_y(3.0, "base");\nfig.save_svg("figure.svg");'),
        dict(id='base_xy', text='The base with positions: the second overload takes it after the data.',
             code='auto fig = studio("ribbon_base_xy");\nauto& pane = fig.pane2("p");\npane.ribbon(std::vector<double>{0, 1, 3, 4, 8, 9}, std::vector<double>{2, 4, 3, 5, 4, 6}, 1.0);\nfig.save_svg("figure.svg");'),
        dict(id='negative', text='Negative values lie below the base, which is zero by default.',
             code='auto fig = studio("ribbon_negative");\nauto& pane = fig.pane2("p");\npane.ribbon(std::vector<double>{2, -1, 3, -2, 4});\nfig.save_svg("figure.svg");'),
        dict(id='matrix', text='A rank-2 `y` makes one band for each column, **stacked**: the rows are the positions, and each band is drawn on top of the ones before it, so its upper edge is the running total. Here two columns give two bands; the upper edge of the second is the sum of the two.',
             code='auto fig = studio("ribbon_matrix");\nauto& pane = fig.pane2("p");\nauto y = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{2, 1, 4, 2, 3, 3, 5, 2}, {4, 2});\npane.ribbon(y);\nstd::cout << "traces: " << pane.trace_count() << "\\n";\nfig.save_svg("figure.svg");'),
        dict(id='errors', text='Inputs that cannot be drawn are refused when the trace is added, and the message names the method and the problem. The pane keeps the traces it had.',
             code='auto report = [](const char* what, auto&& call) {\n  auto fig = studio("ribbon_errors");\n  auto& pane = fig.pane2("p");\n  try {\n    call(pane);\n    std::cout << what << ": accepted\\n";\n  } catch (const pluss::Error& e) {\n    std::cout << what << ": " << e.message() << "\\n";\n  }\n};\nreport("x and y differ", [](Pane& pane) { pane.ribbon(std::vector<double>{1, 2, 3}, std::vector<double>{1, 2}); });\nauto fig = studio("ribbon_errors_out");\nauto& pane = fig.pane2("p");\npane.trend(std::vector<double>{0, 1}, std::vector<double>{0, 1});\nfig.save_svg("figure.svg");'),
        dict(id='wrong_pane', text='`ribbon` needs a planar pane. On a volumetric pane it throws at once, naming the kind it needs, and nothing is added.',
             code='auto fig = studio("ribbon_wrong_pane");\nauto& pane = fig.pane3("p");\ntry {\n  pane.ribbon(std::vector<double>{1, 2});\n} catch (const pluss::Error& e) {\n  std::cout << e.message() << "\\n";\n}\nstd::cout << "traces: " << pane.trace_count() << "\\n";\nfig.save_svg("figure.svg");'),
    ],
    notes='- Returns the new [`Trace&`](../trace/label.md); the reference stays valid for the life of the pane, and the style calls of the trace ([`label`](../trace/label.md), [`stroke`](../trace/stroke.md), [`width`](../trace/width.md), ...) chain off it.\n- The data is copied into the trace when it is added, so changing the vector afterwards does not change the figure.\n',
    see=['[`Pane::spike`](spike.md): the stems.', '[`Pane::trend`](trend.md): the line.', '[`Pane::columns`](columns.md): bars.'],
)
